WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.349
Model size is often described by parameter count.

00:00:03.349 --> 00:00:09.036
Labels such as 3B, 7B, 8B, 14B, 32B, and 70B indicate billions of parameters,

00:00:09.036 --> 00:00:13.098
although architecture and implementation affect actual memory, quality, and speed.

00:00:13.098 --> 00:00:17.972
Smaller models usually load faster, need less memory, and generate more quickly.

00:00:17.972 --> 00:00:22.034
They can be excellent for classification, rewriting, extraction, short assistance,

00:00:22.034 --> 00:00:25.689
narrow domain workflows, and high-volume automation when evaluated carefully.

00:00:25.689 --> 00:00:29.345
Larger models may offer stronger reasoning, knowledge, instruction following,

00:00:29.345 --> 00:00:33.407
or difficult-task performance, but they require more memory and compute.

00:00:33.407 --> 00:00:37.062
They may also increase latency, power use, deployment cost,

00:00:37.062 --> 00:00:39.093
and the consequences of concurrency.

00:00:39.093 --> 00:00:41.124
Bigger is not always better.

00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:45.998
A large model running mostly on a slow CPU or constantly near

00:00:45.998 --> 00:00:50.873
memory limits may be less useful than a smaller model fully accelerated

00:00:50.873 --> 00:00:53.716
with room for context and other applications.

00:00:53.716 --> 00:00:55.747
Model family matters alongside size.

00:00:55.747 --> 00:00:58.996
Training data, architecture, tokenizer, prompt format, specialization, licensing,

00:00:58.996 --> 00:01:03.464
and alignment can make two similarly sized models behave very differently.

00:01:03.464 --> 00:01:06.307
Compare task results, not parameter labels alone.

00:01:06.307 --> 00:01:09.557
Architecture can also change compute and cache behavior.

00:01:09.557 --> 00:01:15.243
Two models with the same headline parameter count may need different memory, context overhead,

00:01:15.243 --> 00:01:19.711
prompt formats, or runtime support, so size categories remain planning approximations.

00:01:19.711 --> 00:01:21.742
Plan for the whole workload.

00:01:21.742 --> 00:01:27.023
A model that fits for one short prompt may fail with long context,

00:01:27.023 --> 00:01:29.866
vision components, multiple sessions, or large outputs.

00:01:29.866 --> 00:01:34.740
Keep memory headroom for cache, runtime, operating system, and recovery from bursts.
